<h2>1. What Is a Process?</h2>

<p>
A process is a program that is currently running.
</p>

<p>For example, execute:</p>

<pre>sleep 300</pre>

<p>
This starts a process that runs for 300 seconds. Press Ctrl+C to stop it.
</p>

<h2>2. Find Running Processes</h2>

<p>Display running processes:</p>

<pre>ps aux</pre>

<p>Find a process by name:</p>

<pre>pgrep -a sleep</pre>

<p>Display detailed process information:</p>

<pre>ps -ef</pre>

<h2>3. Understand PID and PPID</h2>

<p>
Every process has a unique Process ID (PID). A process can also have a parent process, identified by PPID.
</p>

<pre>ps -eo pid,ppid,comm</pre>

<p>
- PID: ID of the running process.
- PPID: ID of its parent process.
</p>

<h2>4. Monitor Processes</h2>

<p>
Use <code>top</code> to monitor running processes and resource usage.
</p>

<pre>top</pre>

<p>Useful shortcuts:</p>

<ul>
<li><code>P</code> - Sort by CPU usage.</li>
<li><code>M</code> - Sort by memory usage.</li>
<li><code>q</code> - Exit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>5. Process States</h2>

<p>A process can have different states:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>R:</strong> Running or ready to run.</li>
<li><strong>S:</strong> Sleeping and waiting for an event.</li>
<li><strong>D:</strong> Waiting for an I/O operation.</li>
<li><strong>T:</strong> Stopped.</li>
<li><strong>Z:</strong> Zombie process.</li>
</ul>

<p>View process states:</p>

<pre>ps -eo pid,stat,comm</pre>

<h2>6. Background and Foreground Processes</h2>

<p>Start a process in the background:</p>

<pre>sleep 300 &amp;</pre>

<p>View background jobs:</p>

<pre>jobs</pre>

<p>Bring a background job to the foreground:</p>

<pre>fg %1</pre>

<p>Stop a foreground process:</p>

<pre>Ctrl + C</pre>

<p>Pause a foreground process:</p>

<pre>Ctrl + Z</pre>

<p>Resume it in the background:</p>

<pre>bg %1</pre>

<h2>7. Stop and Manage Processes</h2>

<p>Find the process ID:</p>

<pre>pgrep -a sleep</pre>

<p>Stop a process gracefully:</p>

<pre>kill -TERM PID</pre>

<p>Forcefully stop a process:</p>

<pre>kill -9 PID</pre>

<p>Pause a process:</p>

<pre>kill -STOP PID</pre>

<p>Resume a process:</p>

<pre>kill -CONT PID</pre>

<div class="note">
Use SIGTERM first. Use SIGKILL only when a process does not stop normally.
</div>

<h2>8. Process Priority</h2>

<p>
Linux uses process priority to decide how CPU time is shared between processes.
</p>

<p>Start a process with a lower scheduling priority:</p>

<pre>nice -n 10 sleep 300 &amp;</pre>

<p>Change the priority of an existing process:</p>

<pre>renice 15 -p PID</pre>

<h2>9. Real-Time Project: Process Monitoring Utility</h2>

<div class="project">
<strong>Project objective</strong>

<p>
Create a Bash script that starts an application, checks whether it is running and automatically restarts it if it stops unexpectedly.
</p>
</div>

<h3>Step 1: Install required tools</h3>

<p>
The following commands are provided by the procps package. Install it before continuing.
</p>

<pre>
if [ "$(id -u)" -eq 0 ]; then
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y procps
else
    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install -y procps
fi
</pre>

<h3>Step 2: Create the project directory</h3>

<pre>
mkdir -p ~/process-monitor
cd ~/process-monitor
</pre>

<h3>Step 3: Create a sample application</h3>

<pre>
cat &gt; app.sh &lt;&lt;'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

while true
do
    echo "$(date): Application is running"
    read -r -t 5 input || true
done
EOF
</pre>

<h3>Step 4: Create the monitoring script</h3>

<pre>
cat &gt; monitor.sh &lt;&lt;'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" &amp;&amp; pwd)"
APP="$DIR/app.sh"
PID_FILE="$DIR/app.pid"
LOG_FILE="$DIR/monitor.log"

is_running() {
    if [ ! -f "$PID_FILE" ]; then
        return 1
    fi

    PID=$(cat "$PID_FILE")

    if ! [[ "$PID" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        return 1
    fi

    kill -0 "$PID" 2&gt;/dev/null
}

start_app() {
    if is_running; then
        echo "Application is already running."
        return
    fi

    rm -f "$PID_FILE"

    bash "$APP" &gt;&gt; "$DIR/app.log" 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;
    echo $! &gt; "$PID_FILE"

    echo "$(date): Application started." | tee -a "$LOG_FILE"
}

stop_app() {
    if is_running; then
        PID=$(cat "$PID_FILE")
        kill -TERM "$PID"
        rm -f "$PID_FILE"

        echo "$(date): Application stopped." | tee -a "$LOG_FILE"
    else
        rm -f "$PID_FILE"
        echo "Application is not running."
    fi
}

status_app() {
    if is_running; then
        echo "Application is running. PID: $(cat "$PID_FILE")"
    else
        echo "Application is not running."
    fi
}

monitor_app() {
    echo "Monitoring started. Press Ctrl+C to exit."

    while true
    do
        if ! is_running; then
            echo "$(date): Application stopped. Restarting." | tee -a "$LOG_FILE"
            rm -f "$PID_FILE"
            start_app
        fi

        read -r -t 5 input || true
    done
}

case "$1" in
    start) start_app ;;
    stop) stop_app ;;
    status) status_app ;;
    monitor) monitor_app ;;
    *)
        echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|status|monitor}"
        ;;
esac
EOF
</pre>

<h3>Step 5: Make the scripts executable</h3>

<pre>chmod +x app.sh monitor.sh</pre>

<h3>Step 6: Start the application</h3>

<pre>./monitor.sh start</pre>

<h3>Step 7: Check application status</h3>

<pre>./monitor.sh status</pre>

<h3>Step 8: Start monitoring</h3>

<pre>./monitor.sh monitor</pre>

<p>
Keep this terminal running. Open another terminal for the next step.
</p>

<h3>Step 9: Simulate application failure</h3>

